        "original_id": "16c2fdff-4d1d-56fc-b7c9-df441a4b0978",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Herbert Lionel Matthews, Fidel Castro, Fulgencio Batista, Ruby Hart Phillips\n- Organizations: The New York Times, 26th of July Movement\n- Places: Sierra Maestra, Cuba\n- Roles: reporter, editorialist, foreign correspondent, president, dictator\n- Events: Cuban Revolution\n- Topics: media coverage of Batista and Castro\n\nFacts:\n- Herbert Lionel Matthews was born on January 10, 1900, and later worked as a reporter and editorialist for The New York Times.\n- Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar, born Rubén Zaldívar, was born on January 16, 1901.\n- Fulgencio Batista was the elected President of Cuba from 1940 to 1944.\n- During Batista's rule, Herbert Matthews became notorious after revealing that Fidel Castro was alive in the Sierra Maestra mountains after Batista had publicly claimed Castro was killed during the 26th of July Movement's landing.\n- Ruby Hart Phillips's relatively favorable coverage of Batista was often at odds with that of Herbert Matthews, the noted Times foreign correspondent who favored Castro.\n- Fulgencio Batista was a U.S.-backed dictator of Cuba from 1952 to 1959 before being overthrown during the Cuban Revolution.\n- Fulgencio Batista died on August 6, 1973.\n- Herbert Lionel Matthews died on July 30, 1977.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "23430ad4-58d8-5119-8a5a-1cdd80eb63df",
        "text": "Page: Herbert Matthews. Sentence: Herbert Lionel Matthews (January 10, 1900 – July 30, 1977) was a reporter and editorialist for \"The New York Times\" who grew to notoriety after revealing that Fidel Castro was still alive and living in the Sierra Maestra mountains, though Fulgencio Batista had claimed publicly that he was killed during the 26th of July Movement's landing.\nPage: Fulgencio Batista. Sentence: Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar (] ; born Rubén Zaldívar; January 16, 1901 – August 6, 1973) was the elected President of Cuba from 1940 to 1944, and U.S.-backed dictator from 1952 to 1959, before being overthrown during the Cuban Revolution.\nPage: Ruby Hart Phillips. Sentence: Her coverage, relatively favorable toward Batista, was often at odds with that of Herbert Matthews, the noted \"Times\" foreign correspondent who favored Castro.",
